background:
[Iliana Iotova](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova) became [Bulgaria's first female president](https://www.bta.bg/en/news/bulgaria/1049637-iliana-iotova-becomes-bulgaria-s-acting-president-after-constitutional-court-rul) on January 23, 2026 after Rumen Radev resigned. Radev would months later form the Progressive Bulgaria party, win parliamentary elections and [become Prime Minister](https://www.politico.eu/article/rumen-radev-sweeps-into-power-bulgaria-names-new-prime-minister/).

Bulgaria's next presidential elections are scheduled for October 2026. [A poll](https://m.novinite.com/articles/239845/Poll+Puts+Iliana+Yotova+Ahead+in+Bulgaria's+Presidential+Race) conducted in late June and early July 2026 showed "around half of respondents said they would be likely to support her". Then on July 24, 2026, [Iotova announced](https://www.bta.bg/en/news/bulgaria/1173487-head-of-state-iliana-iotova-to-run-for-president-in-upcoming-election) her intention to run for presidency because she believes she has "the support of many people in Bulgaria". President Iotova has [the highest approval rating](https://www.ibnaeu.com/en/2026/08/07/rumen-radev-first-100-days-government-bulgaria-poll/) among Bulgaria's senior state officials.&#x20;

Iotova served as the country's Vice President from January 2017 until her ascent to presidency.
